<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paulo Dias de Novais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1510–<a href="/years/1589">1589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paulo Dias de Novais was a Portuguese nobleman and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> who founded the colony of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. Arriving in 1575 with a royal charter to conquer the Kingdom of Ndongo, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in 1576 and began the long wars with Ndongo that fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais.jpg" alt="Paulo Dias de Novais" width="249" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Angola · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Angola_-_1948_-_Colnect_329380_-_Paulo_Dias_de_Novais_1510-1589_colonizer.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paulo Dias de Novais was a fidalgo of the Royal Household. In the 1550s the ruling ngola, or king, of Ndongo broke his allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> and sought recognition from Portugal instead. Hoping for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, slaves, converts and a colony, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> sent an expedition in 1560 under Dias de Novais and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary Father Gouveia. Both were captured and held in Ndongo for several years, until the ngola who had received them died and his successor proved unfriendly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Letters sent from Ndongo by the captives drew royal attention to the country by stressing the supposed mineral wealth of the interior and the advantages of an overland route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> past the gold mines of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The young King Sebastian, eager to promote expansion overseas, granted Dias de Novais a royal charter in 1575 that made him donatário, or donee, of the territory. He landed on 11 February 1575 and, drawn by the prospect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of Cambambe, founded the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> near the island of Luanda.</p><p>As the first Captain-Governor of Portuguese Angola he sought to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> hides, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and above all slaves from the land. Under orders from King Sebastian to keep peaceful relations, he made an alliance with the ngola of Ndongo, Nzinga Ngola Kilombo Kia Kasenda, who in return for military aid sold rebels, prisoners of war and subjects condemned for theft or adultery to the Portuguese as slaves, to his own great profit. Dias de Novais had paid for the Angola venture from his own purse and died bankrupt on 9 May 1589; he was buried in a simple tomb in front of the church of Massangano.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paulo Dias de Novais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…that year in which Ndongo defeated Kongo and won independence; recent research suggests this rests on a misreading of the sources, but Ndongo was now acting on its own account, and in 1560 a Portuguese embassy led by <b>Paulo Dias de Novais</b> came to its court at Kabasa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1575 <b>Paulo Dias de Novais</b> founded São Paulo de Loanda, Luanda, on the coast south of the Kongo Kingdom; Benguela followed in the 17th century, and the town of Mbaka was founded in 1618.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>. Died in Massangano. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
